{"name":"Crushed Stone","hex":"#bcaa9f","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"crushed","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of crush"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Pulverized, rendered into small, disconnected fragments.","Broken, saddened, depressed, devastated."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/crushed"},{"term":"stone","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hard earthen substance that can form rocks; especially, such substance when regarded as a building material.","A piece of such material: a block of stone, rock or a pebble."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Constructed of stone.","Having the appearance of stone."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/stone"}],"entries":[{"title":"Crushed stone","blocks":[{"text":"Crushed stone or angular rock is a form of construction aggregate, typically produced by mining a suitable rock deposit and breaking the removed rock down to the desired size using crushers. It is distinct from naturally occurring gravel, which is produced by natural processes of weathering and erosion and typically has a more rounded shape."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/20mm-aggregate.jpg/330px-20mm-aggregate.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crushed_stone"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/20mm-aggregate.jpg/500px-20mm-aggregate.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/20mm-aggregate.jpg/500px-20mm-aggregate.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"20 mm (0.79 in) crushed stone, used for construction aggregate and landscape applications","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A20mm-aggregate.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wi